@flexiarg or3/skills-that-travel-onward
@title Skills That Travel Onward
@audience practice-based researchers, community engagement leads, workshop designers
@tone formal-analytic
@style design-pattern
@sigils [🧶/传 🚲/行]
@see or3/teach-the-process-not-the-facility, or/citizen-co-learning-track

! conclusion: Skills That Travel Onward

  + context: You run community workshops and are being asked to show what came of them.

  + IF:
    Your practice-based or engagement work brings people together to make, learn, or do something, and its value lies in what participants can do afterwards.

  + HOWEVER:
    The reporting apparatus wants artefacts and attendance figures, so the workshop gets evaluated on what was produced in the room. This pushes designs toward outputs the institution can hold — and away from the thing that actually propagates, which is a skill someone can teach to somebody else next month, in a place nobody is counting.

  + THEN:
    Design explicitly for onward transmission. Favour low-tech, portable techniques that can be re-taught without your equipment or your presence; teach in a way that positions participants as future teachers; and accept that the resulting diffusion will be partly untracked. Document the lineage rather than the inventory — photographs of previous workshops shown at the next one, co-written accounts, interviews — so continuity is visible without enclosing what circulated.

  + BECAUSE:
    A skill that can be re-taught keeps moving after the funding ends, which is the only form of sustainability such work can have. Designing for artefacts optimises for the part that stops when you do.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Ask of each workshop element: could a participant teach this to someone else without us? If not, simplify it.]
    next[Record consent for images separately for participation, publication, and open licensing — see or3/consent-bounded-analysis.]
